<file path="serverlist.md">
 1: # Server list
 2: 
 3: The worlds this client offers on its start screen, one per line:
 4: 
 5: `[VERSION:NAME:DESCRIPTION:LANGUAGE:IMAGE](TARGET)`
 6: 
 7: `VERSION`, `IMAGE` and the description are optional.
 8: 
 9: ## The target
10: 
11: Name your **domain** and the client works the rest out, because it is built
12: once for everyone and cannot have been compiled knowing your addresses:
13: 
14: | | |
15: |---|---|
16: | `play.<domain>` | the client |
17: | `ws.<domain>` | the proxy the browser opens, since it cannot open a TCP socket |
18: | `register.<domain>` | the signup page the login window links to |
19: | `api.<domain>` | the cash shop service |
20: 
21: The connect server is then wherever your proxy reaches it, which is loopback:
22: the two share a machine.
23: 
24: If that is not your layout, publish the addresses instead - `host:port`, and
25: `@` plus your proxy - where `host:port` is the connect server **as the proxy
26: reaches it**, not as a player would:
27: 
28: `[S6EP3:Somewhere:Its own layout:en](10.0.0.4:44405@wss://gate.example.net)`
29: 
30: A world published that way gets no signup link and no shop of its own: it
31: named a game server, not a domain.
32: 
33: ## Naming your game servers
34: 
35: The connect server sends the client ids and load percentages and no text at
36: all, so a world that says nothing here has every game server and every channel
37: on its select screen labelled `Server 1`, `Server 2`. Name them with a list
38: written **directly under your entry**, no blank line in between:
39: 
40: ```
41: - 0: Valhalla
42:   - 0: Peaceful
43:   - 1: Hard
44: - 1: Elysium
45:   - 0: PvP
46: ```
47: 
48: The outer id is the **server group**, `ServerId >> 8` on the wire, and it is
49: `0` unless you set groups up. The inner id is the game server's own id inside
50: that group - the `ServerId` from your server's configuration, which for a
51: single-group world is exactly the number you configured.
52: 
53: Name only what you want named. A world with one group and three channels has
54: nothing to call the group itself, so it leaves that name empty and names the
55: rows:
56: 
57: ```
58: - 0:
59:   - 0: Peaceful
60:   - 1: Hard
61:   - 2: Free PvP
62: ```
63: 
64: Anything left out keeps the numbered default, and so does a world that
65: publishes no list at all. The first line that is not one of these ends the
66: list, which is why the examples above name nothing.
67: 
68: Lines that do not parse are dropped, so the prose around them costs nothing.
69: 
70: [S6EP3:Ignies:Season 6 Episode 3, played in the browser:es:https://github.com/Ignies/OpenMu-Client-Babylon/blob/main/2651842.jpg?raw=true](ignies.net)
71: - 0: Testing Server
72:   - 0: X100 PVP
73:   - 1: X100 PVE
</file>

